      "math_academy_id": "q-72996",
      "sequence_position": 7,
      "problem": "For the functions $e(x)$ and $n(x),$ you're given the following data:\n\n$$\ne(-5)=5,\\,{e}^{′}(-5)=-6,\\,n(-5)=-7,\\,{n}^{′}(-5)=9\n$$\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3e(x)+n(x))$ at $x=-5.$",
      "worked_solution": "Using the sum and constant multiple rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3e(x)+n(x))=3{e}^{′}(x)+{n}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=-5$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3e(x)+n(x))|}_{x=-5} & =3{e}^{′}(-5)+{n}^{′}(-5) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-6)+9 \\\\  & =-18+9 \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
